  "opportunities": [
    {
      "sector": "Resources & critical minerals",
      "anchor": "Coal, iron ore and aluminium alone are A$5,717m — 42.7% of everything Australia sells Vietnam.",
      "why": "Vietnam's factories and construction sector run on imported industrial inputs. Australia is already Vietnam's 9th largest import source but only its 16th largest export market — the relationship is built on Australia supplying the front of Vietnam's value chain.",
      "opportunity": "DFAT's 2040 strategy flags growth in aluminium, copper, zinc and lead, plus exploration services and critical-minerals supply. The Energy and Minerals Ministerial Dialogue announced in 2024 is the vehicle.",
      "risk": "It is also a concentration risk: coal is 26.7% of Australian exports to Vietnam and fell 5.0% year-on-year in the first nine months of 2025 on the Vietnamese ledger. A decarbonising Vietnam is a shrinking coal customer.",
      "sources": [
        "cef",
        "saes",
        "asem"
      ]
    },
    {
      "sector": "Education & skills",
      "anchor": "A$2,658m of education-related travel — 19.9% of Australian exports to Vietnam, the second-largest single line item.",
      "why": "Vietnam is Australia's 4th largest international student market with 36,595 students in 2025, up 4.3%. There are already more than 160,000 Vietnamese alumni of Australian institutions.",
      "opportunity": "Australia and Vietnam have a working group on establishing branch campuses in-country, plus demand in VET, English-language and school segments. Education is the one export line that is a service, not a commodity — it is not exposed to the energy transition.",
      "risk": "Competition for international students is strong, and Australian student-visa settings are a policy variable outside the bilateral relationship.",
      "sources": [
        "cef",
        "brief",
        "saes"
      ]
    },
    {
      "sector": "Agrifood value chains",
      "anchor": "Wheat A$593m and cotton A$593m out of Australia; seafood, coffee, cashews and wood products back the other way.",
      "why": "Australia sells raw agricultural inputs; Vietnam processes them. SunRice's Dong Thap mill — its first direct investment in Vietnam, alongside an ACIAR-backed Mekong Delta sustainable rice value chain project — is the template for moving from trade to investment.",
      "opportunity": "Vietnam's Ministry of Finance met SunRice's CEO in Sydney on 10 August 2026 to discuss expanding rice value-chain investment. Premium food and beverage demand grows with the middle class; halal-certified and ready-to-eat products are named growth lines.",
      "risk": "Market access for fresh produce still moves protocol by protocol on both sides.",
      "sources": [
        "cef",
        "saes",
        "vnplus_20b"
      ]
    },
    {
      "sector": "Green energy transition",
      "anchor": "Vietnam has committed to net zero by 2050 while growing at 8.0% a year.",
      "why": "Those two commitments cannot both be met on the current energy mix. Vietnam needs capital and capability in solar, wind and storage — precisely where Australian funds and engineering firms have depth. Corio Generation (Macquarie Asset Management) is already in Vietnamese offshore wind.",
      "opportunity": "The A$2bn Southeast Asia Investment Financing Facility, run by Export Finance Australia, exists to de-risk exactly this. Australia has also committed A$75m specifically for Vietnam's green transition.",
      "risk": "DFAT's own strategy names the blocker plainly: Vietnam's regulatory and investment framework and complicated approval processes remain obstacles.",
      "sources": [
        "saes",
        "pm2bn",
        "brief"
      ]
    },
    {
      "sector": "Digital economy & services",
      "anchor": "A Memorandum of Understanding on Cooperation in the Digital Economy sits under the Comprehensive Strategic Partnership.",
      "why": "Services are where the investment relationship is thinnest and the margins are highest. A landing pad in Ho Chi Minh City exists to push Australian technology-services exports into the region.",
      "opportunity": "Named growth areas include fintech and digital banking, electronic payments, online education, healthcare and telecommunications — plus professional, financial and logistics services.",
      "risk": "None of this shows up in the A$921m ABS stock yet. This is the pipeline, not the record.",
      "sources": [
        "brief",
        "csp",
        "vnplus_inv"
      ]
    },
    {
      "sector": "Tourism & people flows",
      "anchor": "Recreational travel is Australia's single largest import from Vietnam at A$2,993m — 18.1% of the total.",
      "why": "519,780 Australian resident returns from Vietnam in 2025 make it Australia's 9th most popular destination, and a 326,630-strong Vietnamese-born community anchors the traffic in both directions.",
      "opportunity": "New direct flights, tourism promotion, and building Australian industry capability to serve Vietnamese visitors are explicit action-plan items. Diaspora networks are named in the strategy as an investment-awareness channel.",
      "risk": "Travel is the most cyclical line in the whole account and the first to move in a downturn.",
      "sources": [
        "cef",
        "brief",
        "saes"
      ]
    }
  ],
